              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SummerRain"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I more than doubled the marinade for 2 tuna steaks, added 1 teaspoon brown sugar, a bit of oyster sauce, a dash of sesame oil, and chopped chives. Boiled the leftover of the marinade and thickened it to pour over the grilled tuna (I did pour more water in and seasoned it to make more sauce)."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Valerie Weiler-Hinch"
            },
            "reviewBody": "YUM!!! VERY flavorful marinade. I added 1/2 tablespoon of brown sugar and a bit of sesame oil to the marinade, which I had the tuna in for about 4 hours. Weather forced us to switch from the outdoor grill to stove top, but for my first time grilling tuna steaks, this was a great intro!!",
            "datePublished": "2014-06-29T18:41:09.827Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "REESERCAT"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe is great! I like it better than the teriyaki tuna at my favorite Japanese restaurant. I double the marinade and put half of it aside (before marinating) to use as a sauce when served. I use sashimi grade tuna and grill until medium.",
            "datePublished": "2007-05-11T12:39:08.350Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "chrisb"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We just made this and loved it! It was just the right amount of flavor...delicious marinade, but it didn't overpower the flavor of the fish at all. Just right! I followed the advise of some other reviews...grilled each side 3 minutes, and added green onions and a pinch of brown sugar to the marinade. Also, I didn't have any rice wine, so instead I used a slash of red wine vinegar, and it was great!",
            "datePublished": "2014-06-21T20:05:13.290Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "lauren eyth"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent, simple, and quick go to tuna recipe. One of my boyfriends favorite fish dishes! We both rate it 5 star as written. Feel free to use garlic and/or ginger powder. I add sesame oil instead of vegetable oil into the marinade.",
            "datePublished": "2015-09-04T11:13:52.303Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "ghamilton"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I brushed with olive oil, coated with black pepper and seared 21/2 min per side. I made a sauce to eat with it which consisted of soy sauce, worchestershire, teriyaki, salt and powdered sugar.",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-06T01:38:37.037Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Teresa"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good marinade. I used the amount of marinade for 4 steaks but only had 2. Be sure if you like yours cooked rare or medium rare to only leave it on each side about 2 mintues. I did the first side for 3 and the second for 4 and it was fully cooked and almost starting to get dry.",
            "datePublished": "2009-02-09T17:02:26.373Z"
          },
          {
